\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces The left panel shows an example reconnection region with a single field line identified in red. The guide field is in the $\phi $ direction. For low guide field, the field line has a very small radius of curvature, $R_B$, since the field line is sharply bent and $\rho _e \gg R_B$. In this case the electron does not conserve magnetic moment and the distribution function is isotropized. In the case of high guide field, the electron gyroradius is small relative to the radius of curvature and the pressure anisotropy is conserved.}}{8}{figure.caption.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:field_line_curvature}{{1.2}{8}{The left panel shows an example reconnection region with a single field line identified in red. The guide field is in the $\phi $ direction. For low guide field, the field line has a very small radius of curvature, $R_B$, since the field line is sharply bent and $\rho _e \gg R_B$. In this case the electron does not conserve magnetic moment and the distribution function is isotropized. In the case of high guide field, the electron gyroradius is small relative to the radius of curvature and the pressure anisotropy is conserved}{figure.caption.11}{}}
