\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.5}{\ignorespaces A CAD diagram of the Big Red Ball. The experiment starts with a background field generated by a Helmholtz coil (1) into which plasma is injected via a set of plasma guns (2). After some time, the plasma has equilibrated and then a reverse field is driven via either a set of individual coils or a drive cylinder (3). A reconnecting current layer travels radially inward from high radius to low, passing probes along the way giving us the ability to trace the plasma parameters from one inflow, into an outflow, and into the other inflow.}}{12}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:brb_cad_rendering}{{1.5}{12}{A CAD diagram of the Big Red Ball. The experiment starts with a background field generated by a Helmholtz coil (1) into which plasma is injected via a set of plasma guns (2). After some time, the plasma has equilibrated and then a reverse field is driven via either a set of individual coils or a drive cylinder (3). A reconnecting current layer travels radially inward from high radius to low, passing probes along the way giving us the ability to trace the plasma parameters from one inflow, into an outflow, and into the other inflow}{figure.caption.14}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {1.3.1}Experiment Timeline}{12}{subsection.1.3.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.6}{\ignorespaces Timeline of a single shot for TREX. The permanent magnets are just that, permanent, and thus always on. About four seconds before the start of the shot, the Helmholtz coil is turned on to allow the field to penetrate the three inch thick aluminum wall. Plasma is injected at 12 ms along with an optional toroidal field coil. The drive coils are then pulsed with a ringing current. The first quarter period is of interest to us where first a pressure wave travels inwards, followed by a shock, and finally the reconnection layer.}}{13}{figure.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:experimental_sequence_timeline}{{1.6}{13}{Timeline of a single shot for TREX. The permanent magnets are just that, permanent, and thus always on. About four seconds before the start of the shot, the Helmholtz coil is turned on to allow the field to penetrate the three inch thick aluminum wall. Plasma is injected at 12 ms along with an optional toroidal field coil. The drive coils are then pulsed with a ringing current. The first quarter period is of interest to us where first a pressure wave travels inwards, followed by a shock, and finally the reconnection layer}{figure.caption.15}{}}
